en lines 18 and 19, but execution will eventually resume at line 19. Therefore, this operation is always executed.\n    *   **Conclusion**: The read operation on line 19 is **executable**.\n\n### Summary\n\nAll read and write operations listed in the original defect report are located in unconditionally executed code paths. The preemption of the `main` function by the ISR between lines 18 and 19 is a valid scenario, as there are no interrupt disabling mechanisms in place. Consequently, the sequence of accesses described in the atomicity violation can occur at runtime. The original defect report is correct.\n\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"myglobal\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Write\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [\"18\", \"8\", \"19\"]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[Expert R2] (Refine the report based on judge feedback, re-check for missed or false defects.)\nAbstain\n\n"
